--TEST--
a `Core` member whose capability the configuration does not grant throws a catchable `RuntimeError` naming that capability — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope`, `rule:config/the-root-is-config-else-nvs-toml-else-the-shipped-defaults`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The file exists and is readable by the account running this case; what is
// missing is the grant, and the refusal has to say so rather than report an
// I/O failure — a program that cannot tell the two apart cannot be fixed by
// its operator.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read('./data/note.txt');
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A configuration that grants a *different* capability, so what the refusal
# names is the one the member needed and not the absence of a file: the tree
# resolves, `fs.write` is granted, and `fs.read` still is not.
[capabilities.fs]
write = ["./out"]
--FILE data/note.txt--
note
--EXPECTF--
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%edata%enote.txt, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
